Keep the keyboard table's positions

Tux Typing's practice learns where a key is from each key pressed, through
a switch that knows the US keyboard's keys only: a key it does not know,
such as the ñ, the comma, the full stop or the hyphen of a Spanish
keyboard, is given no position and no finger the first time it is
pressed, so that the hand's finger is no longer lit for it in that run.
The theme's keyboard.lst is the truth about where a key is: learning only
fills in what the table left out, and teaches nothing from a key the
switch does not know. A character 0, which a dead key gives, is no key of
the table; the empty entries after the table's last matched it.
diff --git a/src/alphabet.c b/src/alphabet.c
index 18fc7ad..35ae89d 100644
--- a/src/alphabet.c
+++ b/src/alphabet.c
@@ -270,6 +270,11 @@ int GetIndex(wchar_t uni_char)
 {
   int i = 0;
 
+  /* No character, as a dead key gives, is no key of the table; the empty */
+  /* entries after its last would otherwise match it.                     */
+  if (uni_char == 0)
+    return -1;
+
   while ((i < MAX_UNICODES)
      &&  (keyboard_list[i].unicode_value != uni_char))
   {
@@ -1267,6 +1272,13 @@ void GenerateKeyboard(SDL_Surface* keyboard)
 
 void updatekeylist(int key, char ch)
 {
+  /* The theme's keyboard.lst is the truth about where a key is: learning */
+  /* from a key pressed only fills in what the table left out, and a key  */
+  /* the US layout has not, such as the Spanish n with a tilde, which the */
+  /* practice passes as -1 whatever char's sign, teaches nothing.         */
+  if (key < 0 || key >= MAX_UNICODES || ch == (char) -1
+   || strcmp(keyboard_list[key].keyname, "None") != 0)
+    return;
   keyboard_list[key].latin_char = ch;
   wchar_t wtmp = ch;
   map_keys(wtmp, &keyboard_list[key]);
